# What docker may see when it builds this adapter's image. Everything, minus everything.
#
# `.bessemer/Dockerfile` has no `COPY` and no `ADD`: it installs packages and creates a user, and
# it needs no file from this directory at all. So the honest context is empty, and the two lines
# below say that — deny all, then name the one file the build is. The second line is belt and
# braces rather than load bearing: docker finds the Dockerfile whether or not a pattern excludes
# it, and it is written out so that a reader of the first line is not left wondering.
#
# **What it buys, stated exactly, because the obvious claim is not the true one.** Measured on
# this repository with BuildKit (`docker build … .bessemer`): the build runs *no* context-load
# step at all, with or without this file, because nothing in the Dockerfile asks for one. So the
# claim is not "this makes the build fast today".
#
# The claim is about what the context *is* when something does ask. The build context is the
# adapter directory, and at dispatch time that directory holds `.env` — the gitignored LLM
# credential — `config.local.toml`, the logs, and every live run's `checkouts/<slug>`, each a full
# checkout of this repository. The first `COPY` anyone adds, the classic builder, or a
# BuildKit frontend that reads the context for any other reason, each turns that from a
# hypothetical into a transfer. "Nothing copies the credential in" is a weaker property than "the
# credential was never sent", and only the second one survives an edit to the Dockerfile.
*
!Dockerfile
